calibrate asks the model about labelled records, compares each answer with the label a --label jq expression takes from the record, and prints cut, agreement and confusion tables to pick a gate from. It never picks the cut.

Map only the text a person would read, since a --map that selects the label flatters the question.

Labels: a yes/no label is true, false, yes, no, 1 or 0 in any case. A pick or rate label is an option or level name exactly as declared, or a number whose text is one, so 4 matches --rate 1,2,3,4,5. null, no result or an empty string leaves a record unlabelled for that question, and a record no question labels is not asked. A jsonl record reads {"id":"T-1","body":"the site is down","is_urgent":true}, and a csv one has the header id,body,is_urgent and the row T-1,the site is down,yes.

A question with no --label is skipped, left out of the requests and the report, and named on stderr. At least one question needs a --label, a --label naming no question exits 2, and so does a --require that reads a skipped question. A --resume into an --out file that answers every question still asks the skipped ones, so the file stays whole.

A yes/no cut row flags a record when its value is at least the cut, so the cut goes into a gate as written. A question file's assert, abstain_if, threshold, min_confidence and fallback are ignored, since calibrate reports every cut. The same flags are refused.

A pick or rate table has a row per name. found is the share of the records labelled with a name that were picked as it, and right when picked the share of the records picked as a name that carry it as their label.

--out keeps the answers as -o json lines, and --resume, which needs --id, asks only the records the file does not answer. Changing a label or --cuts reuses every stored answer. A plain stream run can resume the file too, given the same questions, model, -i, --map and --id, with -o json and no gate or merge.

--require turns the report into a check. Each one reads [lower|upper](ID.MEASURE) OP NUMBER [at CUT], where MEASURE is catches, false_alarms, right_when_flagged or auc for yes/no, agreement for pick and rate, and within_one for rate. OP is >=, >, <= or <, and NUMBER is a fraction between 0 and 1. lower and upper compare an end of the 95 percent interval instead of the value, so 16 of 16 fails lower(x.catches) >= 0.9. A yes/no measure other than auc is read at one cut: at CUT names it, at abstain takes it from the -f file's abstain_if, and otherwise the file's assert gives it when it compares ID.value < X or ID.value >= X. The report still prints, and each requirement that did not hold is named on stderr.

--offline, with --out and --resume, reads every answer from the file and asks nothing, so it needs no key and never rewrites the file. A record the file does not answer, a missing file or a file whose fingerprint differs exits 2.

Exit codes:
  0    every record was answered and every --require held
  1    a --require did not hold
  2    usage or validation error, including a bad label or record
  3    the key was refused, the account is out of credits, the keychain failed, or profiles.json or credentials.json is exposed. No report prints
  6    the report holds one or more failed records, which wins over 1
  130  interrupted. No report prints

Usage:
  onesie calibrate [question] [flags]

Examples:
  onesie calibrate --ask urgent='is this urgent' -i jsonl --map '.body' \
      --label urgent='.is_urgent' --id '.id' --out answers.jsonl --resume < labelled.jsonl
  onesie calibrate -f triage -i jsonl --map '.body' --label urgent='.is_urgent' --id '.id' \
      --out answers.jsonl --resume --offline --require 'urgent.catches >= 0.95' < labelled.jsonl

Flags:
      --allow-http                send the API key over a plain http base URL to a host other than this machine. Without it such a base URL exits 2 before any request
      --ask string                NAME=QUESTION, repeatable, opens a question group
      --base-url string           api root override
      --box                       making this a box question, answered with the left, top, right and bottom edges of what it finds in an image, each from 0 to 1. Takes no value. Experimental on TypeSafe's side
      --cache                     store responses on disk and answer a repeated request from them. Also ONESIE_CACHE=1
      --cuts string               comma separated cuts between 0 and 1. Defaults to 0.05 to 0.95 in steps of 0.05
      --desc string               KEY=TEXT, describing one option, level, yes or no
  -f, --file string               question file or request body, or the name of one saved in .onesie/questions or the config dir, or of a built-in starter set
  -h, --help                      help for calibrate
      --id string                 jq expression run on each record, whose string or number result names it
      --image stringArray         NAME=PATH, repeatable, puts a PNG or JPEG in every record's state under NAME. Experimental on TypeSafe's side
      --image-field stringArray   repeatable, a state key whose value is a path to a PNG or JPEG, read per record. Experimental on TypeSafe's side
  -i, --input string              required, jsonl, csv or tsv
  -j, --jobs int                  records in flight at once (default 1)
      --label stringArray         [ID=]EXPR, a jq expression run on the record, not on the mapped state, whose result is the right answer for question ID. A question with none is skipped. With one question ID= may be left out
      --long-context              raise the context limits to 262k tokens, slower and less accurate. Also ONESIE_LONG_CONTEXT=1. Experimental on TypeSafe's side
      --map string                jq expression run on each record, whose result is the state sent. Map only the text a person would read
      --max-retry-after int       most seconds to wait out a server Retry-After (default 60)
  -m, --model string              model override
      --offline                   with --out and --resume, read every answer from the file and ask nothing, needing no key
      --out string                write the answers to this file as -o json lines
  -o, --output string             the report, table, json or auto, which means table
      --pick string               comma separated options, making this a choice question. A repeated --pick adds to the options
      --print-request             write api shaped request bodies to stdout and exit
      --prune                     with --resume, drop the answered ids the input no longer has
      --rate string               comma separated levels in ascending order, making this a score question. A repeated --rate adds levels after the earlier ones. Its score is the level index averaged over p, from 0, and norm is score over the top index, from 0 to 1
      --replace                   --ask overrides an id from -f
      --require stringArray       [lower|upper](ID.MEASURE) OP NUMBER [at CUT], a requirement the report must meet, or calibrate exits 1. Repeatable
      --resume                    with --out and --id, skip the ids the file already answers
      --retries int               retries after a failed attempt (default 2)
      --sep string                separator for --pick and --rate that follow it
      --skip-blank                drop blank lines
      --stats                     write a one line summary of the run to stderr at exit
      --timeout int               seconds per attempt, 60 for a request with images unless given (default 10)
      --usage                     add the api usage object to the answers file and the json report

Global Flags:
      --mock string       answer from this file instead of the API. Also ONESIE_MOCK. The file is an object keyed by question id, as {"answer":0.9,"team":"billing"}, or one line per record in the shape -o json writes. A lone line that carries id, line, model, usage, assert, abstain or an error object is one record
      --profile string    the profile a run uses, from profiles.json in the config dir. Also ONESIE_PROFILE, and otherwise the default profile, which onesie profile use sets
      --provider string   run on this provider without a profile, typesafe, openrouter, berget, levanto or cloudflare. Overrides ONESIE_PROVIDER
